<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han–Xiongnu War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from 129 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Han–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> War was a long series of conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and the Xiongnu confederation, entering its offensive phase from 129 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>. Han armies campaigned deep into the steppe and secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>. The war reshaped the northern frontier and opened routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, though at great financial cost to the Han.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-xiongnu-war.jpg" alt="Han–Xiongnu War" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhangQianTravels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Chinese states had clashed with the steppe peoples who later became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and northern states built long walls against their raids. In 215 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-tian">Meng Tian</a> drove the Xiongnu out of the Ordos region, but in the civil war after the fall of the Qin the Xiongnu, now united in a large nomadic empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a>, returned. Emperor Gaozu of Han was trapped in an ambush at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baideng">Battle of Baideng</a>, and decades of formal peace followed, kept by marriage alliances while the Xiongnu still raided the borderlands.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, the seventh Han sovereign, abandoned this policy for an effort to remove the northern threat for good. In 133 BCE a Han plan to lure Junchen Chanyu into an ambush at Mayi with wealth and promises of defections failed, and raids on both sides grew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In late 129 BCE a Han force of 40,000 cavalrymen made a surprise attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> at the frontier markets. Between 127 and 119 BCE the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> led a series of large campaigns: Wei Qing took the Ordos in 127 BCE, where about 100,000 people were resettled in the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of Wuyuan and Shuofang, and Huo Qubing drove the Xiongnu from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains">Qilian Mountains</a> in 121 BCE. A decisive campaign in 119 BCE pushed them north beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a>.</p><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> in Han hands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> were set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> by 115 BCE and the walls were extended west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>. Loulan, Jushi, Luntai, Dayuan and Kangju were brought into tributary submission between 108 and 101 BCE, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was established in 60 BCE. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-civil-war">Xiongnu civil war</a> broke out in 57 BCE; Huhanye <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">Chanyu</a> submitted to the Han in 53 BCE, and in 36 BCE Chen Tang and Gan Yanshou killed his rival Zhizhi Chanyu at his capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After 46 CE a succession dispute split the confederation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a>, and the southern ruler Bi submitted to the Han in 50 CE. In 89 CE General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-xian">Dou Xian</a> led 40,000 troops against the Northern Xiongnu, chased the Northern Chanyu into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> and accepted the surrender of 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> from 81 tribes, destroying the Xiongnu state. His victory inscription on Mount Yanran was rediscovered in the Khangai Mountains of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in 2017 by a joint Sino-Mongolian expedition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han–Xiongnu wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
